    <h2>Most of Cullman County Runs on a Well</h2>

    <p>Inside Cullman's city limits you're on municipal water, and plumbing looks like it does anywhere. Drive ten minutes toward Baileyton, Holly Pond, or Vinemont and the house is drawing from a private well — a submersible pump sitting a couple hundred feet down, feeding a pressure tank in the crawlspace or the well house. That system runs quietly for years and then fails in ways a city plumber rarely sees. A submersible pump lasts roughly 15 to 25 years, runs on a dedicated 240-volt circuit, and when it goes, you've got no water at all until someone pulls it. Rural water is its own trade, and it's the majority of the plumbing that actually happens in this county.</p>

    <h2>The Pressure-Tank Trap</h2>

    <p>Here's the failure that costs Cullman homeowners the most money, and it's a misdiagnosis. The pump runs constantly, the pressure surges and drops, the faucets stutter — and the reflex is "the well pump is dying." Usually it isn't. The pressure tank has an air bladder that gives the pump something to push against, and when that bladder fails the tank goes waterlogged. Now the pump has no cushion, so it short-cycles: on, off, on, off, every few seconds. That rapid cycling is precisely what destroys a pump. What goes wrong is a homeowner pays to pull and replace the pump down the well — the expensive job — while the real culprit, a $250-to-$500 pressure tank sitting right there at the surface, gets left in place to burn out the new pump too. The practitioner move is to check the tank's air charge first. It takes five minutes and saves the whole misadventure.</p>

    <h2>Septic Is on the Homeowner's Clock</h2>

    <p>The other half of rural plumbing is what leaves the house. Most of the county is on septic, and a septic system is a maintenance item people forget exists until it announces itself. A tank should be pumped every 3 to 5 years; skip it and the solids eventually carry out into the drain field. Here's the honest, unwelcome truth a good plumber will tell you: a tank pump-out is a few hundred dollars, but a drain field that's been ruined by neglect can't be un-ruined cheaply — replacing a field is a five-figure excavation project. There's no magic additive that fixes a clogged field. The only cheap septic is the one you maintained. When drains all through the house slow at once and the yard over the tank smells or stays soggy, that's the system talking, and it's a call-now situation.</p>

    <p>Rural water quality is part of the job too — a lot of Cullman well water carries iron or sulfur, which stains fixtures and smells, and treatment is a real service most homeowners don't know to ask for. A plumber who works wells here will often coordinate with an <a href="/locations/cullman/electrician/">electrician</a> on the well's 240-volt circuit and pressure switch, because the water side and the power side of a well fail together more often than you'd think. It's also the line between real plumbing and a <a href="/locations/cullman/handyman/">handyman</a> call — a leaky hose bib is handyman-sized, but a well pump or a drain field is not.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Plumbing Work Costs in Cullman</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and why they move:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Service call / diagnostic</strong> — about $90 to $175, often credited toward the repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Pressure tank replacement</strong> — roughly $400 to $900 installed; the fix people skip while wrongly replacing the pump.</li>
      <li><strong>Submersible well pump</strong> — about $1,200 to $2,800 pulled and replaced, driven by well depth.</li>
      <li><strong>Septic tank pump-out</strong> — around $300 to $600; cheap insurance against a five-figure drain field.</li>
