    </ul>

    <p>The well and septic numbers swing hard with depth and access, which is why a straight plumber wants eyes on the setup before quoting — anyone who quotes a well pump sight-unseen is guessing at the depth.</p>

    <h2>When to Call Instead of Wait</h2>

    <p>A short-cycling pump is a call-today problem — every rapid cycle shortens the pump's life, so running it "until it quits" just guarantees a full pump replacement instead of a tank swap. Septic on the other hand rewards the calendar: pump it on the 3-to-5-year schedule and you may never see a backup. And if your well water has started staining sinks orange or smelling of sulfur, that's not something to live with — treatment is straightforward once someone tests what's actually in the water.</p>

    <h2>Questions Cullman Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Pump runs constantly and pressure surges — new pump?</strong></p>
    <p>Usually not. It's typically a waterlogged pressure tank, and short-cycling from that is what burns pumps out. Check the tank's air charge before touching the well.</p>

    <p><strong>How often do I pump the septic tank?</strong></p>
    <p>Every 3 to 5 years. Waiting for a backup risks the drain field, which is the expensive part — a pump-out is cheap by comparison.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Service call $90 to $175, pressure tank $400 to $900, well pump $1,200 to $2,800, septic pump-out $300 to $600.</p>

